## Code Example
```javascript
function evalFlag(userId, flagConfig) {
  // basic stub for evaluation
  if (!flagConfig || flagConfig.disabled) return false;
  const bucket = hash(userId) % 100;
  return bucket < (flagConfig.rollout || 0);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle flaky cohort assignment in CI?
- How would you monitor flag drift after deployment?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests end-to-end reliability of streaming reconciliation systems, focusing on critical aspects like idempotency, exactly-once semantics, drift detection, and rollback mechanisms under high throughput conditions—essential for fintech and large-scale payment systems.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end testing in streaming pipelines
- Idempotent reconciliation and exactly-once semantics
- Drift detection and rollback/replay strategies
- Test data generation for duplicates and out-of-order events
- Performance validation at scale (1M events/sec)

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: synthetic event generator with duplicates
function genEvents(n){
  const events=[];
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
    events.push({id: i, timestamp: Date.now(), amount: Math.random()*1000});
    if(Math.random()<0.1) events.push(events[events.length-1]); // 10% duplicates
  }
  return events.sort(() => Math.random()-0.5); // Shuffle for out-of-order
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests privacy-sensitive data sharing across tenants, with explicit consent, retention policies, and data residency constraints. This assesses end-to-end testing, data lineage, masking, and the ability to fail closed (rollback) on privacy signals.

## Key Concepts
- Privacy-preserving data sharing
- Consent management and per-tenant gating
- End-to-end and contract testing across API, event bus, and warehouse
- Data lineage, masking, retention and residency rules
- Drift detection and rollback/kill-switch criteria

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-test sketch: verify per-tenant consent gating before data is emitted
test('consent_gate prevents emission when not given', () => {
  const tenant = {id:'t1', consent:true};
  const data = extractMetrics(tenant);
  expect(emitAllowed(tenant, data)).toBe(true);
  tenant.consent = false;
  expect(emitAllowed(tenant, data)).toBe(false);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test data-residency constraints across regions with a multi-region warehouse?
- How would you simulate a privacy violation and verify rollback gates and audit logs?

